WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.600
Question 0026: three friends and one motorcycle,
a junior-level question.

2
00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:08.767
One part: the shortest time,
the plan, and a general formula.

3
00:00:09.567 --> 00:00:14.767
Three friends at A must get to B.
They have one motorcycle: a driver and one passenger.

4
00:00:14.767 --> 00:00:19.867
Walking the whole way: 13 hours.
Riding the whole way: one hour.

5
00:00:19.867 --> 00:00:23.867
What is the shortest time until all three are at B?
The distance itself is unknown.

6
00:00:24.667 --> 00:00:29.867
Let's start with the first plan that comes to mind.
Above, the road; below, a chart of place against time.

7
00:00:29.867 --> 00:00:35.067
The driver takes the passenger to B,
in one hour. On the chart, a steep line.

8
00:00:35.067 --> 00:00:40.267
He rides back empty to A, another hour,
and the walker waits there all that time.

9
00:00:40.267 --> 00:00:45.467
Then he takes the walker. 3 hours in all,
and the walker wasted two hours waiting.

10
00:00:47.067 --> 00:00:52.267
An improvement: the walker sets out at once.
On the chart, a nearly flat line: slow walking.

11
00:00:52.267 --> 00:00:57.300
The motorcycle rides back and meets him sooner,
because they move toward each other.

12
00:00:57.300 --> 00:01:02.400
So they arrive after 19/7 hours,
about 2.71. But now the passenger waits at B.

13
00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:07.633
The passenger's line is flat at B for almost two hours:
time he could have spent walking.

14
00:01:08.433 --> 00:01:13.233
The idea: nobody needs to wait.
The passenger gets off before B and walks on.

15
00:01:13.233 --> 00:01:18.633
Stage 1: the driver and the passenger ride to D,
seven eighths of the way. The walker reaches Q.

16
00:01:18.633 --> 00:01:24.300
Stage 2: the passenger walks from D; the motorcycle returns
and meets the walker at C, one eighth of the way.

17
00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:29.533
Stage 3: the driver takes the walker to B,
and the passenger arrives on foot at the same moment.

18
00:01:29.533 --> 00:01:34.267
All three are at B after two and a half hours,
and nobody waited for a moment.

19
00:01:34.267 --> 00:01:39.767
The two flat pieces are parallel and equal:
each walks one eighth and rides seven eighths.

20
00:01:40.567 --> 00:01:45.967
Now the formula. Call the ratio of the speeds
α: how many times faster the motorcycle is.

21
00:01:46.467 --> 00:01:51.867
The points cut the road into five pieces:
y, x, z, x and r, in order from A.

22
00:01:51.867 --> 00:01:57.367
Stage 1: in the same time the motorcycle covers y + x + z,
the walker only y. α times faster: equation 1.

23
00:01:57.367 --> 00:02:02.333
Stage 2: the motorcycle goes back z, the walker moves x,
in the same time. So z = αx.

24
00:02:02.333 --> 00:02:06.333
Note: the walker does not stand still.
They close in at v₁ + v₀, not v₁.

25
00:02:06.333 --> 00:02:11.433
Stage 3: the motorcycle covers z + x + r
while the passenger walks r. That is equation 3.

26
00:02:11.433 --> 00:02:16.733
Put z = αx into equations 1 and 3,
and both give the same result: y = r.

27
00:02:16.733 --> 00:02:20.733
The symmetry comes out by itself: the walker walks y + x
at the start, the passenger x + r at the end.

28
00:02:21.333 --> 00:02:25.867
Add up the five pieces, and write
the length of the whole road in terms of x.

29
00:02:25.867 --> 00:02:30.167
So the walker walks y + x, that is
two over α + 3 of the road.

30
00:02:30.167 --> 00:02:35.067
The rest he rides. His total time,
which is everyone's time: T.

31
00:02:35.867 --> 00:02:40.900
Put α = t₀ / t₁, and the distance drops out:
the formula depends only on the two times.

32
00:02:40.900 --> 00:02:46.300
A check with 13 and 1: forty over sixteen,
two and a half hours, as on the chart.

33
00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:52.067
A sanity check at the edges:
a motorcycle as slow as walking does not help at all.

34
00:02:52.067 --> 00:02:57.233
And a very fast motorcycle covers the road three times:
forward, back and forward.

35
00:02:58.033 --> 00:03:02.567
This is the box on the page:
the road, and the same chart under it.

36
00:03:02.567 --> 00:03:07.233
At the end of stage 1: the passenger gets off at D,
the walker is at Q, after 0.875 hours.

37
00:03:07.233 --> 00:03:11.833
At the end of stage 2: the motorcycle picks up
the walker at C, after 1.625 hours.

38
00:03:11.833 --> 00:03:15.133
And at the end of stage 3 all three are at B,
after 2.5 hours.

39
00:03:15.733 --> 00:03:19.833
In the box you can change the times.
This is the plan with 13 and 1.

40
00:03:19.833 --> 00:03:25.300
With 4 hours of walking the passenger gets off earlier,
and the time is 13/7, about 1.86 hours.

41
00:03:25.300 --> 00:03:30.767
And with three who need a ride, each rides
the same distance: three hours and 40 minutes.

42
00:03:31.367 --> 00:03:36.867
Why exactly D? In the proof step you move
the drop point and see two arrival times.

43
00:03:36.867 --> 00:03:40.533
An early drop: the passenger walks a lot
and arrives last.

44
00:03:40.533 --> 00:03:44.933
A late drop: the motorcycle rides back from far away,
and the walker arrives last.

45
00:03:44.933 --> 00:03:50.067
The later of the two is earliest when they are equal:
at the point D, seven eighths of the way.

46
00:03:50.864 --> 00:03:56.464
To sum up: the passenger gets off on the way,
and all three arrive together after two and a half hours.

47
00:03:56.464 --> 00:04:02.364
Try other times in the box
on the page of question 0026 on the site.
